export const GUIDES = {"business":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about business / corporate</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Have you worked with businesses at my stage and size?</li><li>Flat fee or hourly for this piece of work?</li><li>What will you need from me to keep the cost down?</li><li>Who handles it if this later turns into a dispute?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Defined tasks, incorporation, a shareholder agreement, a lease review, are commonly flat-fee. Ongoing or transactional work is hourly. Some firms offer a monthly arrangement for steady advisory work.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>Your incorporation documents if you have them, any agreement you are being asked to sign, and a plain description of what you are trying to achieve.</p></div>","criminal":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about criminal charges</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Have you handled this specific charge before, and how did those matters end?</li><li>What are the realistic outcomes here, including the ones I will not like?</li><li>Who will actually appear in court, you or someone else at the firm?</li><li>What happens to my case if it goes past the stage your quote covers?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Criminal defence is usually billed as a block fee tied to a stage of the case, bail hearing, resolution discussions, trial, rather than one price for everything. Get in writing which stage your fee covers.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>Your release documents or promise to appear, the disclosure package if the Crown has provided it, and the date of your next court appearance.</p></div>","injury":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about personal injury / accident</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Do you work on contingency, and what percentage, calculated before or after disbursements?</li><li>Who covers costs like medical reports if the claim does not succeed?</li><li>How many matters like mine have you resolved, and how long did they take?</li><li>Will you handle my file personally?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Almost always contingency: no fee unless you recover. The number that matters is not just the percentage but whether it is calculated before or after disbursements, and who is liable for those if you lose.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>Accident and police reports, all medical records and treatment notes, correspondence from any insurer, and a record of income you have lost.</p></div>","litigation":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about civil lawsuit / litigation</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>What is a realistic budget through each stage, not just to start?</li><li>What are the odds honestly, and what is my exposure if I lose?</li><li>Would mediation get me a comparable result for less?</li><li>How long is something like this likely to take?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Hourly against a retainer is standard. Because litigation runs long, the useful question is not the hourly rate but the estimated cost to reach each stage, and the risk of paying the other side's costs if you lose.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>The contract or documents at the centre of the dispute, correspondence between the parties, and a dated timeline of what happened.</p></div>","realestate":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about buying or selling a home</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>What is your all-in closing fee, including disbursements and title insurance?</li><li>How far before closing do you need the signed agreement?</li><li>Will you review the status certificate if this is a condo?</li><li>Who do I reach on closing day if something goes wrong?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Residential closings are almost always a flat fee, and it is entirely reasonable to ask for the all-in number in writing before you retain anyone. Ask specifically whether disbursements and title insurance are inside or outside that figure.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>The signed agreement of purchase and sale, your mortgage commitment letter, and your lender's contact.</p></div>","employment":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about employment dispute</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Is the severance I have been offered reasonable for my age, role, and years of service?</li><li>What is the deadline I need to worry about?</li><li>Do you offer a flat-fee review before I commit to anything larger?</li><li>What is the realistic best and worst outcome if we push back?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Many employment lawyers offer a fixed-fee severance review, which is a cheap way to find out whether an offer is low. Negotiation or litigation beyond that is hourly, sometimes partly contingency.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>Your employment contract, the termination letter and severance offer, recent pay statements, and your start date.</p></div>","family":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about family law / divorce</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Do you push toward settlement or mediation first, or straight to court?</li><li>What is your realistic estimate of total cost for a matter like mine?</li><li>How are decisions about the children handled if we cannot agree?</li><li>How quickly do you respond, and who do I contact when you are in court?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Family work is usually hourly against a retainer you top up as it is drawn down. Uncontested separations are sometimes offered as a flat fee. The cost gap between a negotiated agreement and a contested trial is very large, so ask about both.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>Dates of marriage and separation, a rough list of assets and debts, recent pay information for both parties, and any agreement already signed.</p></div>","wills":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about wills, estates &amp; power of attorney</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Do I need a will, an enduring power of attorney and a personal directive, and what does each one do?</li><li>Can one lawyer handle my will, my enduring power of attorney and my personal directive together?</li><li>If I later need help with a family or property matter, could the same firm act?</li><li>What will it cost to draft, and what could it cost my executor to administer the estate later?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Wills and powers of attorney are commonly flat-fee, often priced as a package. Estate administration and probate are usually hourly or a quoted fixed fee, so ask which applies and get it in writing.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>A list of your assets and debts, the people you want as executor, guardian and attorney, any existing documents, and beneficiary designations on insurance and registered accounts. If you already have a will, bring the original or a copy so the firm can tell you what needs updating.</p></div>","immigration":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about immigration</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Have you handled this exact program recently, and what is the current processing reality?</li><li>What is the total cost including government fees?</li><li>What happens if the application is refused, is a re-application included?</li><li>Are you a lawyer or a licensed consultant?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Usually a flat fee per application, quoted separately from government processing fees. Confirm whether responding to a request for more documents is included.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>Passports for everyone applying, education and work records, any prior application or refusal letters, and your current status documents.</p></div>","traffic":"<div class=\"guide\"><h2>Before you call about a traffic ticket</h2><p class=\"g-sub\">General guidance for this kind of matter, not advice about your situation and not specific to this firm.</p><h3>Questions worth asking</h3><ul><li>Have you handled this specific offence before, and what happened?</li><li>Is an early resolution realistic, or is this likely headed to trial?</li><li>Does a conviction here affect my licence or insurance?</li><li>Who appears in court for me, you or someone else at the firm?</li></ul><h3>How this work is usually billed</h3><p>Traffic matters are commonly billed as a flat fee per charge, sometimes bundled if more than one ticket came from the same stop. Ask whether court appearances beyond the first are included in that fee.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><p>The ticket or notice itself, your licence, and the date of your first appearance or trial.</p></div>"};
